A||N||23||newfie||What's so good about the '88??||||5||
Z||000000||newfie||07-09-2000||04:48 PM||||I'm kinda of new with fiero's so I would like the answer to a couple q's that might seem stupid to everyone else.  I have heard the 1988 Fiero's are much better than any previous years.  Due to the upgraded suspension I guess.  Is this true for all models or just the GT.  I know for a 88 SE for sale at a good price.  Is this 88 model any better than previous years.  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||07-09-2000||05:45 PM||||The main differences are improved brakes and redesigned suspension. The other thing is that the 88 models seem to hold their value a little better than the rest of the Fieros.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||07-09-2000||05:48 PM||||First there is no 88 SE there is an SC (sport coupe) and a Formula. The suspension was redesigned for 88 (all 88's) and the brakes were better on the 88's (vented rotors) 88 GT's and Formula's had a rear sway bar added I don't think this was on the base or sport coupe. But part prices are higher for an 88 since it was the last year of the Fiero's that had the redesign. You can make the earlier Fiero's handle as well or better than a stock 88. 88 GT's and Formula's are the most popular Fiero's and the T-Top option on either one is Highly popular. In my opinion ANY year Fiero is a good one and worth purchasing (if the price is right) Is the one you are looking at a V-6 or a 4cyl? V-6's were in the GT and Formula. The base and sport coupe had a 4 cylinder.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||88formula||07-09-2000||07:01 PM||||The 88 fiero's have a completely different suspension than the 84-87 models years. The 84-87 model years use the front suspension from a Chevy Citation in the rear of the fiero and the front suspension from a Chevy Chevette in the front of the fiero. The Chevette suspension does not have the best geometry for a sports car suspension. It has a large scrub radius, which causes a harsh ride and the steering wheel to kick back over every little bump in the road. The Citation rear suspension was never designed to work as a rear suspension so there were compromises made to make it work in the rear of a fiero. One of those compromises was that the tie-rods that would normally steer the Citation’s steering knuckles had to be permanently attached in the rear of a fiero. This causes the fiero's much hated bump steer. Bump steer causes the rear of the fiero to hop or wonder whenever the car hits a bump in the road, especially in a turn. The 84-87 fiero’s didn’t have a rear sway bar, but you car go to the after market and buy one for the rear. These unworthy suspension systems were used in the fiero because GM didn’t want to invest too much money in the P-Car project in 1983-84 and the fiero was only originally meant to be a commuter car and not a sports car. In 1985 it started its way on to becoming a sports cart with the introduction of the v6.  There are a ton of suspension upgrades to cure the fiero’s suspension flaws and make it better that the 88’s suspension from bump steer correction kits to full blown coil over new suspensions for the 84-87 fiero if you have the finances for it.<P>In 1988 the fiero got a completely redesigned suspension system front and rear as well as four wheel vented disk brakes that is unique to the 88 model year. The 84-87 had four wheel disk brakes but they used solid rotors, which are more prone to brake fade than vented one’s. The front of the 88 got its own SLA (84-87 is SLA also) suspension with longer control arms and shorter spindles to give you a shorter scrub radius. This eliminated most of the unwanted feedback you got with the Chevette suspension and eliminated the need for a steering damper. The rear got a tri-link design, which cut compliance, maximized wheel travel, and allowed for a 22mm stabilizer bar for more rear roll stiffness and more neutral handling. The rear is similar in design to what Mazda uses in the rear of the 626. What did this new suspension system give the 88 over the others? For one the fiero had no more bump steer or harsh ride. It rode a lot better than the 84-87 years (I know because I had an 87 coupe) and they are a lot easier to drive at their limit than the 84-87 years. The 88 fiero also got new hydraulic motor mounts, which cut down the vibrations transmitted to the car. This allowed the rear cradle to be mounted solidly to the car eliminating any movement that could throw the rear suspensions geometry off. The 84-87 model years have rubber cradle bushings and are not mounted solidly to the car. <P>In GM’s 88 Brochure they say the 88’s suspension has “reduced spring rates with very high damping control shocks and struts. Result: excellent body motion control while maintaining ride quality with a well-damped feel.” In the front they say “total suspension travel increased; wheel spindles 30% shorter for reduced wheel kick and a 30% smaller scrub radius; 40% more anti-dive geometry; 20% longer upper control arms, 25% longer lowers; revised pivot points for improved steering; Larger stabilizer bars; 12% shorter turning radius.” In the rear GM says “All new tri-link design with Macpherson struts; 22mm rear stabilizer bar on GT and Formula models; improved anti-dive and anti-squat tendencies; rear wheel travel enhanced for reduced jolt reaction to bumps.” <P><BR>The 88 GT and Formula have the WS6 sports suspension package, which includes bigger tires and 15-inch wheels with 7 inch wide rims in the back (tires are 205/60 front and 215/60 rear), rear sway bar, and stiffer springs and struts. The coupe has softer springs and no rear sway bar. The coupe has the same brakes as the GT and Formula.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||TheFiero64||07-09-2000||08:22 PM||||I love the way my 88 feels. It feels a lot lighter than my other cars. It seems to feel tighter on curvy roads. I can drive it much more aggressively then the others.<P>------------------<BR>Joshua Bumpus<BR>New Bedford, MA<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fi.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000005||Monkeyman||07-09-2000||09:10 PM||||Seems to me, after 12 years, everything's gonna be worn out anyway.  If you have to replace parts, you can do it cheaper on a pre-'88 and still end up with a heck of a good handling car.  Grand Am/Beretta brake upgrade costs about $400 (doing the work yourself) and will be at least as good as the stock '88 brakes.  For another few hundred bucks you could upgrade the earlier suspension to perform as well or better than the '88s.  Just my opinion as one of the mechanically challenged.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||07-09-2000||10:20 PM||||88's are nice, I'm sure, but I think you get more bang-for-the-buck with pre-88's due to the inflated prices of 88's and the scarcity of replacement parts at your local auto parts store.<BR> <BR>I do have to take issue with 88formula's charecterization of the handling in pre 88's. From his description, one could deduce that the 84-87 is so twitchy that it should never be driven on anything rougher then a tennis court. I have yet to lose control on dry pavement, and I have not modified my Fiero at all in the suspension dept. I am sure there is room for improvement, but I'm not about to retire my 86 GT!<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||07-09-2000||11:03 PM||||Upgrading the pre-88 suspension for a few hundred bucks to outhandle an '88? HA. Really, with enough money you could make any car outhandle any other car, so in other words its not saying much.  But anyway, I think Car and Driver put it best in their article comparing it with the CRX and MR2, April 1988...<P><BR>    "Have we got a surprise for you.  The new Fiero Formula looks exactly like the flat-footed Pontiac two-seater that has been disappointing us for years-and it did finish last in this test.  But don't laughup your sleeve (or write us a nasty letter) just yet:  the same old body is home to a brand-new soul.  The Fiero elicited oohs and ahs from all four editors.  "This thing is really improved," rejoices Larry Griffin, and he speaks for all of us.<BR>The Fiero acts like a different car now, because that's what it is.  The new suspension Pontiac has been promising us since its debut has finally arrived, and it transforms the Fiero Formula into an agine, confident road eater.  Up front, the ancient pieces inherited from the Chevette have given way to longer control arms.  The X-car hand-me-downs that held up the back have been superseded at each wheel by a strut located by a trailing link and two lateral links.  The anti-roll bars are thicker at both ends.  And all four corners now wear vented brake discs instead of solid ones.<BR>    The Fiero's structure has also been upgraded.  A massive new front subframe, a revised rear subframe, and a host of small changes enhance the chassis's rigidity.  You can feel the improvements every time you bound over a bump or thump across railroad tracks.  The plastic body panels no longer slap against each other when the road jabs at you.  The new Fiero feels as solid as Mount Rushmore.......No, this is not the truculent Fiero of old.<BR>    There is no denying, though, that the Fiero has been reborn.  It's now almost everything it should have been right from the start.....It's a shame that it's taken half a decade to get the Fiero right, but Pontiac and America finally have a budget-priced sports car to be proud of.  Well done."<P>Stimpy, you obviously haven't been looking for a Fiero for sale recently.  I just saw a silver 88 GT in CT for 1,000 advertised on the tech-net.  I bought my old yellow formula for 1,800 and my recent t-top GT for 2,000.  I know of a pretty solid Formula right now for 1,500 (email FieroGTguy@aol.com).  About the "scarcity" of parts, I can't remember the last time any Fiero owner went down to Autozone to find that special Fiero part they needed.<P>Oreif, there was no 88 SC.  There was a sport package (RPO W02) but it wasnt like previous years where the VIN was different. They kept it basic for '88, coupe, formula, GT.  <P>For what its worth, I have driven pre-88s and definitely noticed a difference, kinda like C&D saying it just feels better on the road ("plastic body panels no longer slap..."), more solid.  Maybe some parts are harder to find and/or more expensive, but its minor and insignificant in somparison to the way better road feel and handling you get.  Plus its cool owning a car that is more rare then the average.  Lets get real, earlier Fiero's are a dime a dozen practically.  '88 Fiero's make up 7.13% of all Fiero's made.<P><BR>no flames intended,<BR>jeff<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000008||TOM||07-10-2000||12:09 AM||||so now we are fiero bashing. If its not an 88 its crapola. I think naught,||||reg||1
Z||000009||Ozzy||07-10-2000||01:12 AM||||Just my 2 Aussie dollars worth...<P>I have an '88 Coupe and an '87 GT. The 88 does feel more stable overall. But like Stimpy said, I'm also yet to have the GT bust loose on me. It has a lot of extra body roll and also the infamous bump steer but still hangs on.<P>I have not had any real trouble obtaining parts for the 88 either. Some stuff has been pricey but same with the 87.<P>Both great cars! Whut can I say?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Ozzy||||reg||1
