Z||000011||stimpy||07-10-2000||06:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>Stimpy, you obviously haven't been looking for a Fiero for sale recently.  I just saw a silver 88 GT in CT for 1,000 advertised on the tech-net.  I bought my old yellow formula for 1,800 and my recent t-top GT for 2,000.  I know of a pretty solid Formula right now for 1,500 (email FieroGTguy@aol.com).  About the "scarcity" of parts, I can't remember the last time any Fiero owner went down to Autozone to find that special Fiero part they needed.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Gawd how I hate it when people who don't know me tell me what is obvious about myself. OK, down to brass tacks. First off, if I gave a crap about what Car and Driver recommends, I'd be driving a CRX.<BR>Secondly- I cannot imagine what condition a $1000 88 GT woud be in. I would be shocked out of my pants if it didn't have to be trailered home for some reason. Now, as Cooter has shon, the rare deals are out there, and if I wanted to drive to CT to buy some rusted out frame sight unseen, I too could have an 88 GT. Try looking for an 88 in a sunny state for less then $3000 that don't look or drive rough. There's a chalenge for ya.<P>And I have bought front and rear rotors and calipers for my 86 GT at O'Reilly's. Go ahead, keep fooling yourself that only the Fiero Store carries Fiero parts. I'll keep saving money. <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
